    "md5": "37a47f880c684538ec8c380aed68ce94",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1854,
    "chunk_text": "Welikada gallows ready if death penalty imposed\n\nColombo, Aug. 12 (Daily Mirror) - The Welikada Prison gallows remain ready for use if the government decides to resume executions, with two executioners currently attached to the prison and on standby, the Daily Mirror learns.\n\nThe disclosure comes amid renewed government attention on the possible reintroduction of the death penalty in Sri Lanka.\n\nAccording to a prison official, the two executioners are attached to the Welikada Prison. While they are currently permitted to remain at their respective residences, they are receiving standard salaries and are on standby for duty. The identities of both individuals are being kept strictly confidential for their safety.\n\nThe Welikada Prison in Colombo currently holds Sri Lanka\u2019s only structurally operational gallows. While a second set historically existed at the Bogambara Prison in Kandy, that facility has been closed, leaving Welikada as the sole location maintaining the execution infrastructure.\n\nAccording to prison authorities, 806 inmates are currently on death row. Of them, 58 have been convicted in drug-related cases, comprising 52 males and six females. Prison authorities also said that one foreign national is also among the death row.\n\nThe continued readiness of the prison system has gained renewed significance following recent discussions on the possible reintroduction of capital punishment for drug-related offences. The issue was discussed at the \u201cRatama Ekata\u201d National Operations Council meeting held on Monday (10) at the Presidential Secretariat under the chairmanship of President Anura Kumara Dissanayake.\n\nMeanwhile, the existence of capital punishment in statutory books, though not implemented, also remains a hindrance when seeking the extradition of Sri Lankan organized criminals hiding in European countries."
  },
